  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Intermittent light rain showers, possibly mixed\nwith snow, will impact areas north of Bangor through Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSkies are mostly cloudy this afternoon with temperatures ranging\nfrom around 40 north to around 50 Downeast. The surface low\npressure is well off to our northeast, but another spoke of\nenergy is rotating south around the west side of the low\npressure, and bringing a little light rain mainly to north/east\nAroostook. Expect rain totals to range from a trace to 0.05\nthrough the rest of the afternoon, with precipitation tapering\noff by about 6pm. A few snowflakes could mix in at times, but\ngenerally we are just looking at a cold but light rain.\n\nA new but weak low pressure system moves in from the west\nTuesday, bringing some light rain/snow. Main period of\nprecipitation is from roughly mid-afternoon to early evening,\nand favors the central parts of the region for places like the\nMoosehead Lake area over toward Millinocket, though light\nprecipitation is possible anywhere. Totals should generally be\nless than 0.25 inch, and some areas may get nothing. \nPrecipitation type is a challenge, with many areas of the north \nbeing borderline rain vs rain/snow. That said, think that the \nprecipitation rates will be so light than any snow accumulation \nthrough Tuesday evening will be limited to higher terrain and \nnot impact road travel. As we head later into Tuesday night, the\natmosphere from about Millinocket north will be cold enough to\nsupport just snow, but there will only be a few light showers\nleftover in NW flow by later Tuesday night.\n\nAny showers will be restricted to the northern areas by \nWednesday morning with rain being the primary precipitation type\nwith snow more likely in the northern most areas throughout the\nday. Showers will have dissipated by Wednesday evening, minimum\nsnow accumulations expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for coastal storm development in the \nlater part of next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLarge amounts of uncertainty remain with the low pressure system\ndeveloping in the Mid-Atlantic and moving up the coast this\nweekend. Confidence has increased in a low pressure system\ndeveloping over the Ohio Valley, then intensifying as it moves E\ntowards the Mid-Atlantic. Confidence has also increased in\ncooler temperatures with the system from Friday throughout the\nweekend. Highest uncertainty remains in the track of the low and\nthe impacts that go with it. Any small variation in the track\ncould greatly change the impacts of the system.",
